WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.575
This is an implementation guide,
and it's research from the Federal

3
00:00:04.575 --> 00:00:08.249
Modernization Workbook's Research
section, still under review.

4
00:00:08.249 --> 00:00:13.914
The workbook's DNS, DHCP and IPAM book
sets the program's design for DNS,

5
00:00:13.914 --> 00:00:16.218
DHCP and address management,

6
00:00:16.218 --> 00:00:21.978
and this guide shows how to build that
design on Infoblox NIOS, version 9.1,

7
00:00:21.978 --> 00:00:27.450
and how to move off Microsoft DNS
and DHCP without breaking anything.

8
00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:31.919
After a short orientation, it runs
in four parts: build the Grid,

9
00:00:31.919 --> 00:00:36.212
migrate from Microsoft, sign and
harden, and run it day to day.

10
00:00:36.212 --> 00:00:41.836
Every fact comes from a primary source, the
new ones opened on October twenty-third,

11
00:00:41.836 --> 00:00:45.724
2026, and I'll flag design
guidance and inferences as we go.

12
00:00:45.724 --> 00:00:48.590
The slides and notes carry the detail.

13
00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:54.873
Here's the frame for the whole guide:

14
00:00:54.873 --> 00:00:59.240
the workbook's DNS design, mapped
onto the way Infoblox works.

15
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:04.312
The design wants governed DNS and address
management, and on NIOS that's a Grid,

16
00:01:04.312 --> 00:01:07.403
one Grid Master holding
the database and every

17
00:01:07.403 --> 00:01:10.652
member getting its copy
over an encrypted tunnel.

18
00:01:10.652 --> 00:01:15.463
It wants a period of coexistence with
Microsoft, so NIOS first manages the

19
00:01:15.463 --> 00:01:20.116
Windows servers read-only, then takes
over zones and scopes one at a time.

20
00:01:20.116 --> 00:01:30.292
OMB memo M-08-23 requires DNSSEC, and
NIST SP 800-53's controls SC-20 and SC-21

21
00:01:30.292 --> 00:01:37.515
carry it into every system baseline, so
the Grid Master signs as a hidden primary.

22
00:01:37.515 --> 00:01:40.968
And the design's evidence
becomes syslog, audit logs,

23
00:01:40.968 --> 00:01:43.940
backups and the agency's
settings checklist.

24
00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:49.780
The design's six phases still govern, but
this guide adds a Phase 0 of decisions.

25
00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:58.855
Before you build anything, four decisions,
because each is expensive to reverse.

26
00:01:58.855 --> 00:02:00.730
First, FIPS mode.

27
00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:05.249
You can only turn it on after a factory
reset, turning it off needs another,

28
00:02:05.249 --> 00:02:09.693
and it disables every cloud connection,
including the Infoblox Portal.

29
00:02:09.693 --> 00:02:14.595
Be precise about the claim, too:
NIOS is supported in FIPS mode,

30
00:02:14.595 --> 00:02:20.479
but the FIPS 140-3 and Common Criteria
certifications are still pending.

31
00:02:20.479 --> 00:02:25.092
Running in FIPS mode isn't the same
as holding a validated module.

32
00:02:25.092 --> 00:02:27.610
Second, pick the long-term-support train,

33
00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:30.356
and name it in your
configuration baseline.

34
00:02:30.356 --> 00:02:34.405
Third, fix the weak DNSSEC
defaults before you sign anything.

35
00:02:34.405 --> 00:02:37.327
And fourth, decide whether
the signing keys go in a

36
00:02:37.327 --> 00:02:40.750
hardware security module,
and write the choice down.

37
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.238
Part 1 is building the Grid.

38
00:02:46.238 --> 00:02:50.468
We'll cover the Grid Master pair
and its candidate, how members join,

39
00:02:50.468 --> 00:02:54.391
time and licensing, including
one trap that can evict a member,

40
00:02:54.391 --> 00:02:57.130
and virtual appliances in the clouds.

41
00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.691
The Grid Master comes first,

42
00:03:02.691 --> 00:03:06.393
built as a high-availability
pair, and members come after.

43
00:03:06.393 --> 00:03:09.658
The pair depends on the switch
being set up right, so the physical

44
00:03:09.658 --> 00:03:13.355
cabling and switch-port settings on
the slide matter more than they look.

45
00:03:13.355 --> 00:03:16.105
You run a wizard on node
one to create the Grid,

46
00:03:16.105 --> 00:03:20.489
then join node two with the same name,
virtual address and shared secret,

47
00:03:20.489 --> 00:03:24.205
and manage the pair through the
virtual address from then on.

48
00:03:24.205 --> 00:03:26.986
The step people skip is
the defaults: the login,

49
00:03:26.986 --> 00:03:31.815
the Grid name and the shared secret all
ship with default values you must change,

50
00:03:31.815 --> 00:03:35.693
and escrow the new secret, because
Infoblox says if you forget it,

51
00:03:35.693 --> 00:03:37.742
you have to contact their support.

52
00:03:37.742 --> 00:03:40.470
Next, the backup Grid Master.

53
00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.404
Next comes the Grid Master Candidate,

54
00:03:46.404 --> 00:03:49.645
the appliance that takes over
if the Grid Master is lost.

55
00:03:49.645 --> 00:03:53.114
It holds a full copy of the
database, should be the same model,

56
00:03:53.114 --> 00:03:57.220
and Infoblox gives you a promote test
to check it can reach the members.

57
00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:00.076
Members are defined on
the Grid Master first,

58
00:04:00.076 --> 00:04:04.245
then joined with its virtual address,
Grid name and shared secret.

59
00:04:04.245 --> 00:04:06.908
The ports the Grid needs
are listed on the slide.

60
00:04:06.908 --> 00:04:12.421
The one to watch is FIPS mode, which moves
the Grid's tunnel onto different ports.

61
00:04:12.421 --> 00:04:15.807
So, as an inference,
firewall rules written only

62
00:04:15.807 --> 00:04:19.195
for the usual ports won't
be enough on a FIPS Grid.

63
00:04:19.195 --> 00:04:21.930
Next, two quiet failure modes.

64
00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.017
Two things fail quietly
here: time and licenses.

65
00:04:29.017 --> 00:04:33.568
Set NTP at the Grid level first, with
at least three external servers,

66
00:04:33.568 --> 00:04:36.818
and let the Grid Master
serve time to the members.

67
00:04:36.818 --> 00:04:38.893
Time isn't cosmetic: Grid sync,

68
00:04:38.893 --> 00:04:43.130
Kerberos-signed updates and
DHCP failover all depend on it.

69
00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.903
One gap for your control mapping:

70
00:04:45.903 --> 00:04:50.318
NIOS offers no SHA-2 key
type for NTP authentication.

71
00:04:50.318 --> 00:04:51.543
Then licenses.

72
00:04:51.543 --> 00:04:53.718
The trap is the temporary license.

73
00:04:53.718 --> 00:04:56.071
It lasts sixty days, can't be renewed,

74
00:04:56.071 --> 00:05:01.105
and when it expires the member's services
stop and it's evicted from the Grid.

75
00:05:01.105 --> 00:05:03.793
So never let a production
member run on one.

76
00:05:03.793 --> 00:05:08.830
And if the Microsoft Management license
lapses, sync to the Windows servers stops.

77
00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:11.360
Now, the virtual appliances.

78
00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:20.772
Virtual appliances, vNIOS,
run on VMware, Azure and AWS.

79
00:05:20.772 --> 00:05:24.306
On VMware, the advice is to
leave the VM's settings alone,

80
00:05:24.306 --> 00:05:29.128
because changing them may violate the
licensing terms and stop it joining.

81
00:05:29.128 --> 00:05:35.673
On Azure and AWS, high availability arrived
in 9.0.4, with limits to plan around,

82
00:05:35.673 --> 00:05:42.050
such as both nodes in the same region
and zone, and no IPv6 failover.

83
00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:46.465
Here's the gap that matters for
government: the HA prerequisites list only

84
00:05:46.465 --> 00:05:51.071
commercial cloud endpoints, with nothing
for GovCloud or Azure Government.

85
00:05:51.071 --> 00:05:56.372
So ask Infoblox, in writing, before
you rely on vNIOS failover there.

86
00:05:56.372 --> 00:06:02.380
And one constraint that shapes Part 2: vNIOS
members can't manage Microsoft servers.

87
00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:10.114
Part 2 is the migration from
Microsoft, and it's the longest part,

88
00:06:10.114 --> 00:06:12.643
because it's where a mistake hurts users.

89
00:06:12.643 --> 00:06:16.060
We'll cover managing,
importing, signed updates,

90
00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:21.500
the DHCP takeover and its one
destructive trap, failover, and rollback.

91
00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:29.213
Method A is the one the workbook's
coexistence phase leans on:

92
00:06:29.213 --> 00:06:32.700
manage the Windows servers
first, then take them over.

93
00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:38.493
Grid members manage Windows DNS and DHCP
without an agent, read-only or read-write,

94
00:06:38.493 --> 00:06:43.075
using a physical member with the
Microsoft Management license.

95
00:06:43.075 --> 00:06:47.045
Most zone types and records sync,
but conditional forwarders don't,

96
00:06:47.045 --> 00:06:51.087
so you still need the workbook's
PowerShell export to recreate them.

97
00:06:51.087 --> 00:06:55.947
A few rules bite: each Windows server
syncs into one view, the first sync

98
00:06:55.947 --> 00:07:00.974
belongs off-hours, and a failover
mid-sync loses recent Grid-side changes.

99
00:07:00.974 --> 00:07:05.449
The design guidance is to add the
Windows servers read-only in Phase 1,

100
00:07:05.449 --> 00:07:08.275
so the synced views
become a live inventory.

101
00:07:08.275 --> 00:07:10.140
Then you move the zones.

102
00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:16.515
Method B moves zones by zone transfer.

103
00:07:16.515 --> 00:07:20.469
You let the Windows server transfer
each zone to the Grid Master,

104
00:07:20.469 --> 00:07:24.049
create the same zone on NIOS,
import it, and check the record

105
00:07:24.049 --> 00:07:28.152
counts and answers before you
change any delegation or forwarder.

106
00:07:28.152 --> 00:07:30.264
That's the workbook's cutover loop.

107
00:07:30.264 --> 00:07:32.765
The import brings in the ordinary records,

108
00:07:32.765 --> 00:07:37.408
but recreates the zone's own name server
and start-of-authority records rather

109
00:07:37.408 --> 00:07:42.265
than copying them, which matches the workbook's
rule never to migrate the zone apex.

110
00:07:42.265 --> 00:07:46.015
Subzones come in as delegations,
so import them separately.

111
00:07:46.015 --> 00:07:49.883
For bulk data there's an import
wizard, but Infoblox warns mass

112
00:07:49.883 --> 00:07:53.903
imports can cause a service outage,
so run them in a change window.

113
00:07:53.903 --> 00:07:56.520
Next, Active Directory zones.

114
00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:03.323
Active Directory zones and
Kerberos-signed updates

115
00:08:03.323 --> 00:08:06.445
start with a correction to
the vendor's documentation.

116
00:08:06.445 --> 00:08:11.208
Infoblox's page for generating
the key file shows RC4 encryption.

117
00:08:11.208 --> 00:08:16.695
But Microsoft's July 2026 update
moved Kerberos RC4 protections into

118
00:08:16.695 --> 00:08:23.033
enforcement on domain controllers, so
generate the key file with AES 256 instead.

119
00:08:23.033 --> 00:08:29.259
The workbook's DNS design keeps the AD
locator zones on AD DNS by default until the

120
00:08:29.259 --> 00:08:31.489
last domain controller goes,

121
00:08:31.489 --> 00:08:36.694
and even then NIOS needs AES keys so
its DHCP can send signed updates.

122
00:08:36.694 --> 00:08:40.978
The alternative, NIOS hosting
the AD zone itself, works,

123
00:08:40.978 --> 00:08:46.308
but no Infoblox page names Windows
Server 2025 domain controllers.

124
00:08:46.308 --> 00:08:48.695
Confirm support before you take that path.

125
00:08:48.695 --> 00:08:51.470
Now, the DHCP takeover.

126
00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:58.508
The DHCP takeover has
one destructive trap.

127
00:08:58.508 --> 00:09:03.302
Infoblox documents that removing
a managed Windows DHCP server from

128
00:09:03.302 --> 00:09:07.932
Grid management deletes all its
ranges, leases and fixed addresses.

129
00:09:07.932 --> 00:09:12.344
So take the scopes over first, and
remove the server from management last.

130
00:09:12.344 --> 00:09:16.080
The takeover itself is a handful
of steps that move each range

131
00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:19.595
and fixed address from the
Windows server to a Grid member.

132
00:09:19.595 --> 00:09:24.313
On the client side, Windows clients keep
renewing with the server that issued their

133
00:09:24.313 --> 00:09:28.895
lease, so the old Windows scope has to stop
answering, or clients keep its settings.

134
00:09:28.895 --> 00:09:32.809
Whether a client keeps its
address isn't documented, so,

135
00:09:32.809 --> 00:09:37.070
as an inference, pilot one scope
and lower lease times first.

136
00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:38.690
Then failover.

137
00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:47.640
DHCP failover pairs two servers,
and at least one must be NIOS.

138
00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:52.280
A mixed Windows and NIOS pair isn't
offered, which is why the workbook's

139
00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:57.490
DNS design moves both partners of a
Windows failover relationship together.

140
00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:01.801
Leave the default settings alone unless
you understand the consequences,

141
00:10:01.801 --> 00:10:04.652
and configure everything
before you turn DHCP on.

142
00:10:04.652 --> 00:10:06.720
One common assumption is wrong:

143
00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:11.014
the Grid Master plays no part in
failover; the peers talk directly.

144
00:10:11.014 --> 00:10:13.003
IPv6 is different.

145
00:10:13.003 --> 00:10:17.258
An IPv6 range is served by a
single member with no failover,

146
00:10:17.258 --> 00:10:21.665
so redundancy comes from making that
member a high-availability pair.

147
00:10:21.665 --> 00:10:26.840
That's not a regression, since
Microsoft's DHCPv6 has no failover either.

148
00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:28.550
Now, how to back out.

149
00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:34.200
Every method needs a way back.

150
00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:39.075
Managing read-only is easy to undo,
because nothing on Windows changed.

151
00:10:39.075 --> 00:10:44.812
Read-write is riskier, since switching back
silently reverts recent Grid-side edits.

152
00:10:44.812 --> 00:10:49.682
For zone import, the Windows zone stays
authoritative until you change delegation,

153
00:10:49.682 --> 00:10:53.000
so the workbook's cutover loop
is the real rollback point.

154
00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:57.937
For the DHCP takeover, no Infoblox
rollback procedure was found, so,

155
00:10:57.937 --> 00:11:02.963
as an inference, reverse the steps
and reactivate the Windows scope.

156
00:11:02.963 --> 00:11:07.548
The NIOS database snapshot is a quick
undo for configuration mistakes,

157
00:11:07.548 --> 00:11:11.900
but there's only one, and it isn't
for migration data clients depend on.

158
00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:14.718
Throughout, each phase
ends with a rollback

159
00:11:14.718 --> 00:11:18.200
point and a consistency
test, not a calendar date.

160
00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:24.312
Part 3 is signing and hardening.

161
00:11:24.312 --> 00:11:28.089
We'll cover DNSSEC on NIOS,
the hardware security module,

162
00:11:28.089 --> 00:11:33.013
the agency's own hardening baseline,
FIPS mode, and administrator access,

163
00:11:33.013 --> 00:11:36.980
including two upgrade gates
that can lock people out.

164
00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:45.743
On a Grid, only the Grid Master
signs, acting as a hidden primary.

165
00:11:45.743 --> 00:11:49.181
The key rule is to set the
parameters before you sign anything,

166
00:11:49.181 --> 00:11:50.867
because the defaults are weak.

167
00:11:50.867 --> 00:11:57.362
Change the algorithm from RSA with SHA-1
to SHA-256 or an elliptic curve option,

168
00:11:57.362 --> 00:12:01.725
and raise the zone-signing
key from 1024 bits to 2048,

169
00:12:01.725 --> 00:12:05.581
which matches NIST's
guide for DNS deployment.

170
00:12:05.581 --> 00:12:09.668
The other defaults are fine,
including automatic key rollovers.

171
00:12:09.668 --> 00:12:13.263
One decision to document:
NIST prefers NSEC,

172
00:12:13.263 --> 00:12:18.955
while NIOS defaults to NSEC3, so decide
which you use and write it down.

173
00:12:18.955 --> 00:12:21.784
And if you ever change a
signed zone's algorithm,

174
00:12:21.784 --> 00:12:24.105
add the new one before removing the old.

175
00:12:24.105 --> 00:12:26.170
Next, the HSM.

176
00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:33.657
The hardware security module keeps
the signing keys off the Grid.

177
00:12:33.657 --> 00:12:37.233
NIOS supports Thales Luna
and Entrust nShield.

178
00:12:37.233 --> 00:12:41.443
The important step is registering both
the Grid Master and the candidate,

179
00:12:41.443 --> 00:12:44.894
because the candidate becomes
the signer after a promotion.

180
00:12:44.894 --> 00:12:48.202
Signing moves to the HSM
at the next key rollover,

181
00:12:48.202 --> 00:12:51.757
not immediately, and NIOS
never deletes keys from it.

182
00:12:51.757 --> 00:12:54.545
Then you publish DS
records to the parent zone.

183
00:12:54.545 --> 00:12:58.579
If the parent is in the same
Grid, that's automatic;

184
00:12:58.579 --> 00:13:04.932
otherwise you export them, and, as an
inference, submit only the SHA-256 digest.

185
00:13:04.932 --> 00:13:07.284
For zones already signed on Windows,

186
00:13:07.284 --> 00:13:10.969
sign on NIOS and validate before
you stop the old signing.

187
00:13:10.969 --> 00:13:13.200
Now, the hardening baseline.

188
00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:18.575
How do you harden NIOS?

189
00:13:18.575 --> 00:13:21.843
This workbook is for
Moderate civilian agencies,

190
00:13:21.843 --> 00:13:24.075
so the baseline is the agency's own.

191
00:13:24.075 --> 00:13:29.143
NIST's control C M 6 has you document your
configuration settings for each product,

192
00:13:29.143 --> 00:13:32.050
from a common secure
configuration you choose.

193
00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:37.129
For most products that's a C I S
Benchmark or the vendor's hardening guide,

194
00:13:37.129 --> 00:13:39.712
but there's no C I S Benchmark for NIOS.

195
00:13:39.712 --> 00:13:45.450
So write the settings from NIST's DNS
deployment guide and Infoblox's documentation:

196
00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:49.876
zone transfers only to named
secondaries, with signed transfers;

197
00:13:49.876 --> 00:13:55.041
restricted dynamic updates; authoritative
and recursive roles kept apart;

198
00:13:55.041 --> 00:13:58.319
a hidden primary and
strong signing algorithms;

199
00:13:58.319 --> 00:14:02.500
administrator sign-in with PIV or
SAML; and DNS logs in the SIEM.

200
00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:06.904
The DISA STIG for NIOS 9 is the
only product-specific checklist,

201
00:14:06.904 --> 00:14:11.562
so it makes a useful cross-check,
but it's High only, Defense material.

202
00:14:11.562 --> 00:14:16.225
It covers only the appliance's
DNS, with forty-five rules.

203
00:14:16.225 --> 00:14:18.634
One tip from it applies to any baseline:

204
00:14:18.634 --> 00:14:22.625
check these settings at the zone
level, not only at the Grid level.

205
00:14:22.625 --> 00:14:24.930
Next, FIPS mode.

206
00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:33.656
Turning on FIPS mode is a short
procedure built around a factory reset,

207
00:14:33.656 --> 00:14:36.830
a longer shared secret,
and a self-test reboot.

208
00:14:36.830 --> 00:14:40.835
The order matters: on a high-availability
pair, set it on each node

209
00:14:40.835 --> 00:14:45.217
before forming the pair, and join
special members before you enable it.

210
00:14:45.217 --> 00:14:49.892
After an upgrade, there's one extra
command to make Grid traffic compliant.

211
00:14:49.892 --> 00:14:51.742
What changes is significant.

212
00:14:51.742 --> 00:14:56.536
Only modern TLS stays on, weak algorithms
are flagged, and portal and cloud

213
00:14:56.536 --> 00:15:01.880
connections are disabled, and they aren't
restored if FIPS mode is later turned off.

214
00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:04.380
Threat updates have to
be uploaded by hand.

215
00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:08.417
And remember slide three: the certificates
themselves are still pending.

216
00:15:08.417 --> 00:15:11.030
Next, administrator access.

217
00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:20.321
Administrators should sign in with PIV,
checked by OCSP, or with SAML through Entra,

218
00:15:20.321 --> 00:15:24.417
keeping the local admin as
break-glass for directory outages.

219
00:15:24.417 --> 00:15:26.995
Put the GUI and API on
the management port,

220
00:15:26.995 --> 00:15:30.405
restrict who can reach them,
and add the consent banner.

221
00:15:30.405 --> 00:15:34.955
Now the two gates in the 9.1.0
upgrade that can lock people out.

222
00:15:34.955 --> 00:15:39.133
The test upgrade fails on administrator
usernames that don't comply,

223
00:15:39.133 --> 00:15:43.017
and those users can't log in after
the upgrade, so check them first.

224
00:15:43.017 --> 00:15:46.806
And older TLS versions must be
turned off before you upgrade.

225
00:15:46.806 --> 00:15:51.638
For the settings baseline, the evidence
is an authentication policy with

226
00:15:51.638 --> 00:15:56.155
a remote service, a password policy,
and only the agency's approved CAs.

227
00:15:56.155 --> 00:15:58.040
That closes Part 3.

228
00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:04.578
Part 4 is running it
and producing evidence.

229
00:16:04.578 --> 00:16:09.763
We'll cover upgrades and the point of no
return, patching, backup and recovery,

230
00:16:09.763 --> 00:16:12.973
logging, automation, and
the whole build mapped

231
00:16:12.973 --> 00:16:16.630
onto the workbook's DNS
design and its six phases.

232
00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:26.318
Upgrades follow five steps: download,
upload, distribute, test, and upgrade.

233
00:16:26.318 --> 00:16:30.879
The step to respect is distribution,
because it's the point of no return.

234
00:16:30.879 --> 00:16:34.480
It removes the ability to revert
to the version you had before.

235
00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:37.731
The test upgrade is
mandatory from 9.1.0 onward,

236
00:16:37.731 --> 00:16:41.068
and it catches data errors
before they hurt you.

237
00:16:41.068 --> 00:16:44.695
During the upgrade, avoid
changing or restarting members,

238
00:16:44.695 --> 00:16:49.531
and recreate scheduled tasks afterward,
because a full upgrade deletes them.

239
00:16:49.531 --> 00:16:53.405
Reverting a single member is
possible only in a short window,

240
00:16:53.405 --> 00:16:56.892
and reverting the whole Grid
loses every change since.

241
00:16:56.892 --> 00:17:00.980
As for long-term support, by
inference the current train runs

242
00:17:00.980 --> 00:17:04.904
to about April 2027, so confirm
the approved federal build.

243
00:17:04.904 --> 00:17:06.570
Next, patching.

244
00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:12.120
Patching has a couple of catches.

245
00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:15.027
Hotfixes go to the Grid
Master and every member,

246
00:17:15.027 --> 00:17:18.232
and members that join later
need them applied again.

247
00:17:18.232 --> 00:17:22.746
Common Criteria Grids need a different
upgrade image, so check which one you upload.

248
00:17:22.746 --> 00:17:24.707
The bigger point is what to watch.

249
00:17:24.707 --> 00:17:30.496
CISA's catalog of known exploited
vulnerabilities lists no Infoblox product,

250
00:17:30.496 --> 00:17:35.671
but it does list an old BIND vulnerability,
added on October eighth, 2026.

251
00:17:35.671 --> 00:17:40.972
Because NIOS ships BIND, the program's
patch watch covers BIND advisories too,

252
00:17:40.972 --> 00:17:45.445
not only Infoblox's own notices,
mapped against the release notes.

253
00:17:45.445 --> 00:17:48.110
Next, backup and recovery.

254
00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:54.651
Backup is off by default, so schedule it,

255
00:17:54.651 --> 00:17:59.798
and send it by SCP rather than FTP,
which sends credentials in clear text.

256
00:17:59.798 --> 00:18:03.472
Back up before every
upgrade, restore or revert.

257
00:18:03.472 --> 00:18:08.793
Restoring has a wrinkle: SCP isn't listed
as a restore source, so, as an inference,

258
00:18:08.793 --> 00:18:14.197
copy backups back to the management
system and rehearse restoring from there.

259
00:18:14.197 --> 00:18:18.335
Losing the Grid Master means promoting
the candidate from its console.

260
00:18:18.335 --> 00:18:23.301
Notify members one at a time, which
Infoblox says can minimize DNS outages,

261
00:18:23.301 --> 00:18:26.232
then confirm every member has reconnected,

262
00:18:26.232 --> 00:18:31.198
and update anything that pointed at
the old Grid Master, including the HSM.

263
00:18:31.198 --> 00:18:35.286
Written down, those steps are your
documented promotion procedure.

264
00:18:35.286 --> 00:18:36.770
Next, logging.

265
00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:42.533
Logging is where the evidence comes from.

266
00:18:42.533 --> 00:18:48.108
Send syslog to external servers over
secure TCP, use the detailed audit log,

267
00:18:48.108 --> 00:18:53.145
which also records API calls, and
copy the audit log to syslog as well.

268
00:18:53.145 --> 00:18:57.527
Be careful with full DNS query
logging, because Infoblox warns it

269
00:18:57.527 --> 00:19:02.233
significantly affects performance,
so use capture or dnstap instead.

270
00:19:02.233 --> 00:19:07.182
DHCP lease history is what ties an
address to a device at a point in time.

271
00:19:07.182 --> 00:19:12.619
On a FIPS Grid, the portal-managed
Data Connector is likely unavailable,

272
00:19:12.619 --> 00:19:15.844
as an inference, so rely
on syslog or dnstap.

273
00:19:15.844 --> 00:19:17.828
And the SIEM is the system of record,

274
00:19:17.828 --> 00:19:22.433
because local retention can't meet the
six-month and twelve-month requirement.

275
00:19:22.433 --> 00:19:24.230
Next, automation.

276
00:19:27.980 --> 00:19:30.980
Much of the Grid can be
codified, but not all of it.

277
00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:37.670
Terraform's NIOS provider version 2 needs
NIOS 9.1.0 and covers most of the build,

278
00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:42.405
from members and zones to
DHCP, failover and admin roles.

279
00:19:42.405 --> 00:19:47.229
A new combined provider is in beta
and isn't backward compatible, so,

280
00:19:47.229 --> 00:19:52.317
as an inference, build on version 2
now and plan to re-import state later.

281
00:19:52.317 --> 00:19:56.767
Ansible covers a lot too, but has
nothing for failover or DNSSEC.

282
00:19:56.767 --> 00:20:00.979
And some things stay manual:
FIPS mode, the factory reset,

283
00:20:00.979 --> 00:20:04.917
promoting the candidate,
the HSM and DNSSEC signing.

284
00:20:04.917 --> 00:20:08.229
Write those as runbooks that
capture their evidence as they go.

285
00:20:08.229 --> 00:20:10.610
Now, the whole build in order.

286
00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:19.251
Here's the whole build laid onto the
six phases of the workbook's DNS design,

287
00:20:19.251 --> 00:20:22.298
and the order is the
program's design guidance.

288
00:20:22.298 --> 00:20:24.722
Phase 0 makes the four decisions.

289
00:20:24.722 --> 00:20:29.866
Phase 1 builds a lab Grid, then production
in order, from the Grid Master pair and

290
00:20:29.866 --> 00:20:35.247
candidate to sign-in, logging and backups,
with the Windows servers added read-only.

291
00:20:35.247 --> 00:20:40.173
Phase 2 coexists, adding members
on premises and in each cloud hub.

292
00:20:40.173 --> 00:20:44.126
Phase 3 moves authority zone
by zone and scope by scope,

293
00:20:44.126 --> 00:20:46.410
re-signing signed zones on NIOS.

294
00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:52.360
Phase 4 federates the clouds, with
Universal DDI only on non-FIPS scopes.

295
00:20:52.360 --> 00:20:56.897
Phase 5 decommissions behind the
design's gates, locator zones last.

296
00:20:56.897 --> 00:21:01.820
And Phase 6 finishes the remaining
baseline settings and the evidence.

297
00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:06.495
To close.

298
00:21:06.495 --> 00:21:11.591
The decisions are yours: FIPS mode,
knowing it costs cloud management,

299
00:21:11.591 --> 00:21:16.687
whether the signing keys go in an HSM,
NSEC or NSEC3 for internal zones,

300
00:21:16.687 --> 00:21:22.320
where the locator zones live, and how
many physical managing members you need.

301
00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:25.434
There are questions to put
to Infoblox in writing,

302
00:21:25.434 --> 00:21:29.536
mainly around the approved federal
build, certification timing,

303
00:21:29.536 --> 00:21:33.258
Windows Server 2025, and failover
in the government clouds.

304
00:21:33.258 --> 00:21:37.430
The next steps are concrete: stand
up a lab Grid, sign a test zone,

305
00:21:37.430 --> 00:21:42.420
pilot one DHCP takeover, and check the
lab against your settings baseline.

306
00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:47.252
The program's view is plain: NIOS
can carry the workbook's DNS design,

307
00:21:47.252 --> 00:21:51.908
and its risks are manageable if
the Phase 0 decisions come first.

308
00:21:51.908 --> 00:21:54.948
This is the program's
research, under review,

309
00:21:54.948 --> 00:21:57.820
not an endorsement by any federal agency.

310
00:21:57.820 --> 00:21:58.630
Thank you.
